      "text": "**Design Memo: Local-First Note-Taking App**\n\n**1. Data Model for Notes, Tags, and Links**\n\nThe app will use a simple, relational data model with three main entities: **Notes**, **Tags**, and **Links**.\n\n- **Notes** have a unique ID, title, content, creation date, and last modified date.\n- **Tags** are also uniquely identified and have a name and a color (optional).\n- **Links** connect notes to each other or to tags, with a source note ID, target note ID, or target tag ID, and a type (e.g., \"related\", \"contains\", \"tagged\").\n\nExample:  \nNote A (ID: 123) is tagged with \"Project\" (Tag ID: 456), and Note B (ID: 789) is linked to Note A as \"related\".\n\n---\n\n**2. Full-Text Search (Offline)**\n\nFull-text search should be performed locally using an indexed search engine that supports offline operation. Each note’s content should be indexed in a local database (e.g., SQLite or a similar embedded database), with fields such as title, content, and tags included in the index.\n\nThe search should support fuzzy matching, partial matches, and tag-based filtering. Search results should be sorted by relevance and date modified.\n\nExample:  \nA user searches for \"project deadline\" and gets results including Note A (which contains \"project deadline\") and Note B (which is tagged with \"project\" and mentions \"deadline\").\n\n---\n\n**3. Conflict-Resolution Strategy for Syncing**\n\nWhen two devices are offline and both edit the same note, a **last-write-wins (LWW)** strategy with **timestamp-based resolution** will be used. Each note includes a version number and a timestamp. When syncing, the note with the later timestamp (and higher version number) will overwrite the other.\n\nIn case of a tie (same timestamp), the",
